## 1. Introduction
1,3-Propanediol has wide applications for large volume markets, particularly in polymer business where it is used to produce polytrimethylene terephthalate (PTT), a kind of newly developed polymers with many outstanding properties (Zeng and Biebl, 2002). Chemical synthesis of 1,3-propanediol has been achieved by hydration of acrolein and subsequent catalyzed hydrogenation. However, this production route, based on petroleum industry, is both difficult and costly. Microbial production of 1,3-propanediol, a socially beneficial route to obtain chemicals from renewable resources, has been widely researched and considered as a competitor to the traditional petrochemical routes. Only a narrow range of microorganisms in nature are able to produce 1,3-propanediol by fermenting glycerol, which include Klebsiella sp. (Zheng et al., 2008), Citrobacter sp. (Daniel et al., 1995b) and Clostridium sp. (Gonzalez-Pajuelo et al., 2005). Great interest has been put in Klebsiella pneumoniae because of its powerful ability to produce 1,3-propanediol in a high yield and productivity.
In K. pneumoniae, the anaerobic metabolism of glycerol is a process of dismutation (Zeng and Biebl, 2002). In the oxidative pathway, glycerol is first converted to dihydroxyacetone by a $\mathsf{N A D}^{+}$ -dependent glycerol dehydrogenase, and dihydroxyacetone is phosphorylated by dihydroxyacetone kinase. The generated dihydroxyacetone phosphate is further oxidized in the subsequent metabolism to produce more energy. In the reductive pathway, glycerol is first converted to 3-hydroxypropionaldehyde by glycerol dehydrase and then 3-hydroxypropionaldehyde is reduced to 1,3-propanediol by an NADH-dependent 1,3-propanediol oxidoreductase (1,3-PDOR) (Fig. 1). A consequence of the oxidative process is the generation of excess reducing equivalents in the form of reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NADH). Regeneration of oxidized nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide $(\mathsf{N A D}^{+})$ necessitates the formation of a by-product to serve as an electron receptor in the reductive process.
Fermentation studies of natural strains revealed that 3- hydroxypropionaldehyde was accumulated during fermentation especially under high substrate concentrations (Wang et al., 2003). 3-Hydroxypropionaldehyde has been proved to be a kind of highly toxic compound which would prevent microorganisms from normal growth (Barbirato et al., 1996). The accumulation of 3-hydroxypropionaldehyde indicates that microorganisms need more 1,3-PDOR during fermentation under high substrate concentrations. However, over-expression of 1,3-PDOR in natural strains did not increase the production of 1,3-propanediol although the intracellular concentration of 3-hydroxypropionaldehyde could be kept at a low level (Zhao et al., 2009). In the meanwhile, over-expression of an aldehyde dehydrogenase from E. coli, which utilizes NADPH rather than NADH as coenzyme, led to a high productivity of 1,3-propanediol (Zhang et al., 2006). Aldehyde dehydrogenase has recently been demonstrated to be a kind of protective enzyme in microorganisms and could catalyze the transformation of a wide range of aldehydes to the corresponding alcohols (Pérez et al., 2008), but its catalytic efficiency is lower than that of 1,3-PDOR in catalyzing 3-hydroxypropionaldehyde to 1,3- propanediol. Additionally, our previous metabolic flux analysis of glycerol utilization suggested that the formation of 1,3-propanediol is limited by the amount of NADH supplied by the oxidative pathway and the conversion rate of glycerol would increase if both NADH, supplied by the glycolytic pathway (EMP pathway), and NADPH, supplied by the pentose phosphate pathway (PP pathway), could be utilized by 1,3-PDOR to produce 1,3-propanediol (Zhang et al., 2007). Considering the fact that an additional phosphate group esterified to the $2^{\prime}$ -hydroxyl group of the ribose at the adenine end is the only difference between NADP(H) and NAD(H), it is possible to relax the coenzyme specificity of 1,3-PDOR by rational protein design.
Efforts to alternate the coenzyme specificity of oxidoreductases from different families have been conducted for decades (Nishiyama et al., 1993; Rosell et al., 2003; Tomita et al., 2006). Despite of these efforts, alteration of the coenzyme specificity still remains a challenge due to the lack of understanding of the structural and energy characteristics of the binding of different coenzymes to the same enzyme. In this study, a series of molecular simulations were employed to get a deeper insight into the structural and energy characteristics of the binding of different coenzymes to 1,3-PDOR. Site-directed mutagenesis was carried out to realize the relaxation of the coenzyme specificity.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Homology modeling of 1,3-PDOR with coenzymes
The amino acid sequence of 1,3-PDOR from $K.$ pneumoniae DSM2026 was retrieved from the GenBank database (accession no. AAP97875.1). Multiple sequence alignment was performed and the 3D-models of 1,3-PDOR with coenzyme were generated employing Modeller 9v2, which could model the coenzyme into the binding site using comparative modeling technologies. To increase the accuracy of the calculated structure, loop modeling was performed with the enumeration algorithm. The 3D-models were evaluated by Procheck, Whatif and Prove, available at Biotech Validation Suite for Protein Structures (http://biotech.ebi.ac.uk/).
## 2.2. Molecular dynamics (MD) simulations
Each complex was neutralized by adding sodium counter ions randomly and solvated in a rectangular box of TIP3P water molecules with a solute–wall distance of $10{\mathring{\mathsf{A}}}.$ The solvated systems were energy-minimized prior to the MD simulations. Each system was minimized by six consecutive rounds of 1500 steps. Harmonic constraints were applied to all non-hydrogen atoms with the strength of 500, 400, 300, 200, 100 and $0\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-2}$ at each round. After that, the systems were slowly heated from 0 to $300\mathrm{K}$ in $120{\mathsf p}s$ .
The MD simulations were performed employing Amber 9.0 using the force field developed by Cornell et al. (1995) with a periodic boundary condition in the NPT ensemble at $300\mathrm{K}.$ Langevin dynamics was used with the collision frequency of $1.0{\mathsf{p s}}^{-1}$ and constant pressure of 1 atm. The Shake algorithm was applied to fix all covalent bonds. The Particle Mesh Ewald (PME) method was used to treat long-range electrostatic interactions. A residue-based cutoff of $10\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ was applied to the non-covalent interactions. No constraint was applied during MD simulations which lasted 1270 ps for each complex to get an equilibrium state.
## 2.3. Binding free energy calculations
The MM-GBSA approach was applied to compute the binding free energy $(\Delta G_{\mathrm{binding}})$ . The $\Delta G_{\mathrm{binding}}$ was determined from the free energies of the complex, protein and ligand according to the equation:
$$
\Delta G_{\mathrm{binding}}=G_{\mathrm{water}}\left(\mathrm{complex}\right)-\left[G_{\mathrm{water}}(\mathrm{protein})+G_{\mathrm{water}}(\mathrm{ligand})\right]
$$
The binding free energies for each term in turn were estimated from the absolute molecular mechanical energies $(E_{\mathrm{MM}})$ , the solvation free energies $(G_{s0l})$ and the vibration, rotation and translation entropies.
## 2.4. Computational alanine-scanning mutagenesis
To obtain the mutant residues from the wild type, for each residue belonging to the active site, all atoms in the side chain after carbon $C\gamma$ were deleted and $C\upgamma$ was replaced with a hydrogen atom which was placed at a $1.09\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ distance from the carbon $\mathsf{C}\mathsf{\beta}$ along the $C\upgamma\mathrm{-}C\upbeta$ bond. The energy change between the mutant and the wild type is calculated for each mutation according to the following equation:
$$
\Delta\Delta G_{\mathrm{binding}}=\Delta G_{\mathrm{binding}}\left(\mathrm{mutant}\right)-\Delta G_{\mathrm{binding}}(\mathrm{wildtype})
$$
## 2.5. Clonings of dhaT and dhaT′
The genomic DNA of K. pneumoniae DSM2026 was extracted by CTAB method. The gene of dhaT encoding 1,3- PDOR was amplified using the primers P1 ( $5^{\prime}$ -GCGAGAAGGTATCATATGAGCTATCGTATG- $3^{\prime}$ , introduced Nde I restriction site underlined) and P2 ( $5^{\prime}$ -AAGCGACATGACGGATCCCTCGTTAACAC-3′, introduced BamH I restriction site underlined). The amplified DNA fragment were digested and ligated to the Nde I-BamH I-digested $\mathsf{p E T}23\mathsf{a}(+)$ to produce recombinant plasmid $\mathsf{p E T}23\mathsf{a}(+)$ -dhaT.
The gene of dhaT′, which encodes 1,3-PDOR-D41G, was obtained using $\mathsf{p E T}23\mathsf{a}(+)$ -dhaT as the template through primer overlap extension PCR technique. Firstly, primers P3 $(5^{\prime}\cdot$
TAATACGACTCACTATAGGG- $3^{\prime}$ , T7 promoter sequence) and P4 $5\mathrm{^{\prime}-}$ AGGCCTTTGCCGGTGACCAG- ${\cdot}3^{\prime}$ , introduced mutation site underlined) were used to amplify the $5^{\prime}$ end of dhaT′; P5 $5^{\prime}-$ TGGTCACCGGCAAAGGCCTG- $3^{\prime}$ , introduced mutation site underlined) and P6 $5^{\prime}$ -GCTAGTTATTGCTCAGCGG- $3^{\prime}$ , T7 terminator sequence) for the $3^{\prime}$ end. Secondly, the full-size fragment was amplified using the two overlapping fragments as template and P3, P6 as primers. Then, dhaT′ was digested, ligated, and transferred into E. coli BL21(DE3) through the same procedure as described above.
## 2.6. Purifications of 1,3-PDOR and 1,3-PDOR-D41G
The supernatant of the cell extract was loaded onto Q Sepharose FF ion exchange column. The column was first equilibrated then washed with $100\mathrm{ml}$ of buffer A $50\mathrm{mM}$ Tris–HCl buffer $\mathrm{(pH}~7.4)$ containing $0.1\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}$ and $2\mathrm{mM}$ DTT) at a flow of $2{\bmod{\mathrm{min}}}^{-1}$ . The proteins were eluted with buffer B (buffer A supplemented with 1 M KCl) using a linear gradient of $_{0-1\mathrm{{M}}}$ at a flow rate of $5\mathrm{ml}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . Active fractions were pooled and concentrated. The concentrated enzyme sample was applied on a column of Sephacryl S-300. The column was eluted with buffer A at a flow rate of $0.5\mathrm{ml}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ .
## 2.7. Enzyme activity assays
The activities of enzymes were assayed by monitoring the $A_{340}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . For the reductive reactions, the assay mixture contained $100\mathrm{nM}$ enzyme, $0.37\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{NAD(P)}\mathrm{H},27\mathrm{mM}3\mathrm{-}$ hydroxypropionaldehyde, $30\mathrm{mM}$ ammonium sulfate, and $100\mathrm{mM}$ Tris–HCl buffer $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ . For the oxidative reactions, the assay mixture contained $100\mathrm{nM}$ enzyme, $2\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{NAD}(\mathrm{P})^{+}$ , $100\mathrm{mM}~1,3-$ propanediol, $30\mathrm{mM}$ ammonium sulfate, and $100\mathrm{mM}$ potassium carbonate buffer $(\mathsf{p H9.0})$ .
One unit of enzyme activity was the amount catalyzing the formation of $1\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ of product per minute under the specified conditions. All assays were performed in triplicate and the reported values are the average.
## 2.8. Determination of kinetic parameters
The $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ values obtained were determined at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with Tris–HCl buffer $\left(\mathsf{p H7.4}\right)$ for the reductive reactions or potassium carbonate buffer $(\mathsf{p H9.0})$ for the oxidative reactions. They were determined from the results of experiments in which a fixed concentration of the substrate and an appropriate range of concentrations of the coenzyme were used. The kinetic parameters were calculated by regression to the Michaelis–Menten equation.
## 3. Results
## 3.1. Homology models of 1,3-PDOR with coenzymes
As a result of the Gapped-BLAST-based template search through Protein Data Bank (PDB), 1,3-PDOR from K. pneumoniae DSM2026 has a high level of sequence identity with 1,2-propanediol oxidoreductase of E. coli (PDB code: 2bi4, 2bl4) and lactaldehyde reductase of $E_{\sun}$ . coli (PDB code: 1rrm), with the identical percent being $43\%$ , $43\%$ and $42\%$ , respectively. Thus, the $\mathsf{X}$ -ray crystal structures of 2bi4, 2bl4 and 1rrm were selected as templates to generate the 3D structure of 1,3-PDOR with coenzyme. Nine models were generated through homology modeling. The Ramachandran plot results from Procheck reports that the best model has 300 $(89.6\%)$ residues in the most allowed region, 27 $(8.1\%)$ in the additional allowed region, $5(1.5\%)$ in the generously allowed region, 3 $(0.9\%)$ in the disallowed region. The Whatif and Porve results also show that the 3D models presented here are reasonable. So the best model was chosen as shown in Fig. 2.
## 3.2. Hydrogen-bond analysis of the binding of coenzymes
Since the MD simulations include hydrogen atoms, it is possible to make an analysis of hydrogen-bond properties. Fig. 3 shows the hydrogen-bond interactions between coenzymes and the residues around the active site pocket. It is noteworthy that for NADH, the O of Asp41 forms hydrogen-bonds with the hydroxyl group of the ribose at the adenine moiety, while no hydrogen-bond appears between Asp41 and NADPH because of the additional phosphate group esterified to the $2^{\prime}$ -hydroxyl group of the ribose at the adenine end. The other residues forming hydrogen-bonds with coenzymes are chiefly serine (Ser103 with NADH and NADPH) and threonines (Thr142 with NADH; Thr143 with NADPH; Thr146 with NADH and NADPH). Besides, the backbone nitrogen and oxygen of glycines (Gly102 with NADPH) and leucine (Leu183 with NADH) can also form hydrogen-bonds with coenzymes.
## 3.3. Binding free energy calculations
The overall binding free energies are summarized in Table 1, including all of the energy terms given by the MM-GBSA method for each complex. The results of the overall binding free energies suggest that, compared with NADPH $(-10.08\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1}$ ), NADH $(-50.30\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1},$ ) is the favorable coenzyme for 1,3-PDOR and there are no large energy differences in terms of the “gas phase” van der Waals contribution $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{vdW}})$ and the nonpolar contribution to the solvation free energy $(\Delta G_{\mathrm{np}})$ . The electrostatic energy contribution to the solvation free energy $(\Delta G_{\mathrm{PB}})$ makes 1,3-PDOR prefer NADPH, while the “gas phase” electrostatic energy contribution $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ele}})$ makes 1,3-PDOR prefer NADH. It is evident that the effect of $\Delta E_{\mathrm{ele}}$ is larger than $\Delta G_{\mathrm{PB}}$ , demonstrating that the force for the responsibility of the coenzyme specificity is the “gas phase” electrostatic energy.
## 3.4. Computational alanine-scanning mutagenesis of the active site residues
To elucidate the key residues in the binding site pocket and the favorable interaction mode, computational alanine-scanning mutagenesis was carried out. This method can be considered as an alternative approach to investigate the major contributions by the critical residues. In this study, the active site was defined as the region that consists of residues with any of its atoms within $3\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ of the coenzymes. Fig. 4 illustrates the results of the alaninescanning mutagenesis, from which it is noteworthy that Asp41Ala is not only the mutation with the smallest value of Gbinding, but also the only mutation with a negative $\Delta\Delta G_{\mathrm{binding}}$ . Thus, only the mutation of Asp41 could improve the strength of interactions between 1,3-PDOR and NADPH.
## 3.5. Glycine mutagenesis vs. alanine mutagenesis
Because the computational alanine-scanning mutagenesis depends on the assumption that local changes of the protein do not influence the whole conformation of the complex significantly, molecular dynamics were performed and binding free energies were calculated for the glycine and alanine mutagenesis with NADPH as coenzyme. Overall binding free energies were $-38.79\$ and $-37.84\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1}$ for each mutation, indicating that glycine mutation could further weaken the repulsion between Asp41 and the phosphate group esterified to the $2^{\prime}$ -hydroxyl group of the ribose at the adenine end of NADPH (Fig. 5). Thus, it is suggested that the residue Asp41 be mutated to glycine to relax the coenzyme specificity of 1,3-PDOR to the greatest extent.
## 3.6. Activities and kinetic parameters of the enzymes
Determinations of enzyme activities showed that the specific activities of 1,3-PDOR with NADH and $\mathsf{N A D}^{+}$ are 706.90 and $68.12\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mg}^{-1}$ , respectively. No activities could be determined for 1,3-PDOR with NADPH or $\mathsf{N A D P^{+}}$ . The specific activities of 1,3- PDOR-D41G are 277.35 and $9.63\mathrm{U}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for NADPH and $\mathsf{N A D P^{+}}$ , as well as 629.38 and $47.06\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mg}^{-1}$ for NADH and $\mathsf{N A D}^{+}$ . It is evident that 1,3-PDOR-D41G could catalyze the reaction from 3- hydroxypropionaldehyde to 1,3-propanediol with either NADH or NADPH, implying the successful relaxation of the coenzyme specificity of 1,3-PDOR.
To gain a deeper insight into the relaxation of the coenzyme specificity of 1,3-PDOR, kinetic properties such as $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{max}}$ and $k_{\mathrm{cat}}$ were determined and summarized in Table 2. According to Table 2, the $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value for 1,3-PDOR with NADH is smaller than that for 1,3- PDOR-D41G with NADH and the $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value for 1,3-PDOR-D41G with NADPH is smaller than that with NADH. However, the $k_{\mathrm{cat}}$ value for 1,3-PDOR-D41G with NADH is larger than that for both 1,3- PDOR with NADH and 1,3-PDOR-D41G with NADPH. Additionally, the ratio of $k_{\mathrm{{cat}}}/K_{\mathrm{{m}}}$ indicates that the catalytic efficiency for 1,3- PDOR-D41G with NADPH is higher than that with NADH, giving the preference of 1.56-fold.
## 4. Discussion
1,3-PDOR (EC 1.1.1.202) has been regarded as a member of the $\mathsf{N A D}(\mathsf{P})^{+}$ -dependent alcohol dehydrogenase group III of “iron-activated” dehydrogenase family. Although the amino acid sequences of 1,3-PDOR from the natural strains that have been extensively studied in the fermentation of glycerol to 1,3- propandeiol have been known for a period of time (Zheng et al., 2004; Daniel et al., 1995a), there is only one X-ray crystal structure published to date (Mar ¸cal et al., 2009). However, this crystal structure was determined without coenzyme. Considering the fact that the overall structure will change a lot when coenzyme enters the binding site, especially the structure of the dinucleotide-binding fold, it is not reasonable to use this structure to investigate the interaction between 1,3-PDOR and coenzymes. Fortunately, 1,3- PDOR from K. pneumoniae has a high sequence similarity with 1,2-propanediol oxidoreductase and lactaldehyde reductase of E. coli, which makes the homology modeling of 1,3-PDOR with coenzymes and the subsequent computational simulations possible and reliable.
Hydrogen-bond interaction always plays a significant role in the binding of ligand to protein. For each coenzyme studied here, there are a lot of hydrogen-bonds, indicating the importance of hydrogen-bond interaction in the binding of NAD(P)H to 1,3-PDOR. The hydroxyl group-containing residues around the active site are significant for the binding of coenzymes to 1,3-PDOR. The biggest difference for the two kinds of coenzymes is the hydrogenbonds formed between the O of Asp41 and the hydroxyl group of the ribose at the adenine moiety. A similar hydrogen-bond was found in the X-ray crystal structure of 2bi4, where Asp41 of 1,3-PDOR is replaced by Asp39 (Montella et al., 2005). Sequence alignment suggests that this aspartic acid is conservative in the $\mathsf{N A D}(\mathsf{P})^{+}$ -dependent “iron-activated” alcohol dehydrogenase family, implying its key role in recognizing NAD(H) from NADP(H). The overall binding free energy indicated that the electrostatic energy term was the major force that the discriminated NADH from NADPH. The computational alanine-scanning mutagenesis of the active site residues showed that Asp41 was the key residue responsible for the coenzyme specificity. Combining these discoveries, one could draw the conclusion that due to the large size and strong negative charge of the side chain of Asp41, there are strong stereospecific blockage and electrostatic repulsion between Asp41 and the phosphate group esterified to the $2^{\prime}$ -hydroxyl group of the ribose at the adenine end of NADPH. Mutation of Asp41 to alanine would eliminate the electrostatic repulsion and weaken the stereospecific blockage to a certain extent. Based on the results of the overall binding free energies of Asp41Ala and Asp41Gly mutagenesis, the stereospecific blockage could be further weakened if Asp41 is changed to glycine.
The measured activities for the two coenzymes are consistent with the calculated binding free energies of the wild type and the mutant. According to the determination of $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ , NADH is the preferable coenzyme for 1,3-PDOR, while 1,3-PDOR-D41G prefers NADPH, which means that after elimination of the stereospecific blockage and electrostatic repulsion, the coenzyme binding pocket has changed from being more suitable for NADH to NADPH. Because the $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value for 1,3-PDOR-D41G with NADPH is smaller than that with NADH, the catalytic efficiency for 1,3-PDOR-D41G with NADPH is higher than that with NADH, although the measured activity is higher for 1,3-PDOR-D41G with NADH than that with NADPH. Besides, the catalytic efficiency for 1,3-PDOR-D41G with NADPH is a little higher than that with NADH. This may result from the fact that the coenzyme binding pocket of 1,3-PDOR-D41G facilitates NADPH more than NADH to get into the active site because of their better structural complementarities.
The first description of the NAD(H)-dependent oxidoreductase structure of lactate dehydrogenase was published by Adams et al. (1970). Since then, interest has been put in the outlining of the typical determinants and evolution of nicotinamide binding sites and great efforts have been made in the alteration of the coenzyme specificity of oxidoreductases from different families. It has been known that in oxidoreductases that bind NAD(H), there is a conserved, negatively charged residue (Asp or Glu) at the end of the second strand of the dinucleotide-binding fold (Bellamacina, 1996). The space that would be taken up by the extra phosphate of NADP(H) is occupied by the conserved hydrogen-bond between the Asp or Glu in the binding site and the adenosine ribose of the NAD(H). Thus, in order to alter the coenzyme specificity of NAD(H)-dependent oxidoreductases, this conserved amino acid residue should be changed. In the practice of alcohol dehydrogenase (Rosell et al., 2003) and phosphite dehydrogenase (Woodyer et al., 2003), besides the conserved site, other mutation sites were also introduced to alter the coenzyme specificity. As for malate dehydrogenase (Nishiyama et al., 1993) and lactate dehydrogenase (Tomita et al., 2006), the coenzyme specificity was altered by inserting a short loop that includes the change of the conserved site. In this work, the coenzyme specificity of 1,3-PDOR was successfully relaxed by introducing only one mutation site, presenting a good example of the rational design of protein structure and function as well as laying the foundation for the construction of novel genetically engineered bacteria in the field of 1,3-propanediol production.
